“Must” identifies a required outcome. “Proposed” identifies a design or policy awaiting review. [TODO] identifies missing evidence or a decision. This document specifies requirements; it does not claim that FOG natively implements every automation or that tests have passed.

## 1. Project Background

IT Support currently prepares Windows 11 devices and distributes images using two Clonezilla USB drives and two external image drives. Image restoration alone takes approximately 3–4 hours per device. Volume is approximately 8–10 devices in slow months and over 30 in heavy months, with a largest observed batch of 14.

Preparation currently includes Enterprise activation, Webroot, Office 2016 or Microsoft 365, Chrome, StarTech display drivers, Acrobat Reader, Zoom Workplace, Entra joining, naming, Teams, Resolve, updates, and approved cleanup. Images captured after identity-sensitive configuration have produced duplicate Entra devices, Resolve records overwriting one another, and BitLocker recovery-key confusion.

The project will establish central network imaging and repeatable post-deployment configuration, using existing equipment. Each deployed device must acquire its own identity and encryption state. The desired outcome is faster deployment without trading away identity correctness, Secure Boot, or completed BitLocker encryption.

> Methodology: link each operational problem to a measurable outcome; distinguish machine elapsed time from technician labor.

## 2. Basic Requirement Overview

| Element | Confirmed requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Primary owner | Requesting IT Support technician; also maintains the service |
| Production approval | Director of IT |
| New devices | Dell Inspiron DC15250, DC16250; Dell Slim ECS1250 |
| Typical endpoints | Intel i5 / Core 5, 16 GB RAM, 512 GB M.2 storage; DDR4/DDR5 variants |
| Server | Dell PowerEdge R720, two Intel Xeon CPUs, 32 GB ECC DDR3 |
| Storage | Two WD Purple 6 TB drives in RAID 1; nominal mirrored capacity approximately 6 TB, not 12 TB |
| OS / status | Ubuntu Server 26.04.1 LTS directly on server; initial updates underway; FOG not installed |
| Network | Switch reported as 10 Gb; exact ports, server uplink, DHCP and topology unknown |
| Connections | Laptops through USB Ethernet adapters; desktops through built-in Ethernet |
| Image size | Current images approximately 130–150 GB; actual FOG sizes to be measured |
| Identity | Entra join only; local Active Directory domain join is out of scope |
| Security | Secure Boot enabled; standard employee accounts; completed BitLocker encryption with keys in Entra |

Scenarios:
1. IT Support prepares a batch for incoming employees at the office, chooses the correct model/Office image and approved names, starts deployment, and performs separately timed validation before handoff.
2. IT Support receives an eligible former employee device, verifies the required backup on its external drive, authorizes data removal, and deploys a clean image for the next employee.
3. Monthly, the maintenance owner refreshes and validates all six images, promotes them, and updates the rotating external backup.

## 3. Business Analysis and System Research

| Approach | Value | Limitation / decision |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Existing Clonezilla workflow | Known local recovery method | Two-device practical concurrency; 3–4-hour restore baseline |
| FOG plus post-deployment automation | Central images, network deployment and repeatable setup | Needs network, Secure Boot, identity and performance validation |
| Microsoft provisioning tools | Candidate supported Entra provisioning mechanism | Entitlement, tenant policy and permissions unverified; not a committed solution |
| SharePoint / CSV integration | Potential future assignment lookup | Explicitly outside release one |

Pain-point priority: (1) slow batch delivery, (2) identity and recovery-key correctness, (3) manual repetitive setup, (4) maintaining and recovering consistent images.

No additional hardware or licenses are included. Labor includes setup, pilot testing, automation development, six monthly image refreshes and maintenance. [TODO O9: measure baseline hands-on effort and estimate implementation/maintenance hours.] Time waiting for a copy is not equivalent to technician labor saved. No monetary ROI is asserted.

ROI method: monthly labor benefit = deployment count × measured hands-on minutes saved, less monthly maintenance minutes. Track elapsed-time improvement separately.

## 4. Project Benefit Goals

> Methodology: measurable acceptance gates, with performance hypotheses tested before a production commitment.

| Goal | Target / status |
| --- | --- |
| Deployment elapsed time | Under 60 minutes; under 45 minutes stretch. Includes first boot, setup, updates, reboots and completed BitLocker encryption. Unvalidated target, not a guarantee. |
| Batch capacity | Demonstrate representative batches up to 14 devices on existing equipment; both same-image and mixed-image workloads |
| Identity correctness | Every accepted device has its own verified Entra and agent identities; no overwriting another device |
| Security completion | Secure Boot on; approved volumes fully encrypted, protection on, matching recovery-key association verified in Entra |
| Operational readiness | Six approved images, runbook, trained staff, restore-tested backups and Director approval before production rollout |

### Measurement boundaries

- **Preflight:** connection, backup/erasure authorization, image/name selection, and any first-time firmware certificate enrollment. Record separately.
- **Deployment:** starts when the technician releases the approved deployment task; includes queueing, transfer, first boot, configuration, enrollment, required app/activation steps, updates/reboots and encryption completion. Ends only at Ready for validation.
- **Technician validation:** sign-in and functional testing after deployment. Separate clock and separate target: **[TODO O9: stakeholder to set target]**.
- **Employee sign-in:** employee-specific Microsoft 365 authentication excluded from deployment; applications must be installed and ready.
- Required manual enrollment or remediation is not reclassified as testing to improve the deployment metric. Record such work, elapsed delay and unmet automation requirement.
- Batch elapsed time runs from batch release until the last device is Ready for validation. Report per-device and whole-batch results, image mix, failures, retries and preflight time.
- Stage durations may overlap; total elapsed time is measured independently, not by summing overlapping durations.

### Performance feasibility

Full encryption makes the 45-minute goal more demanding. Server link speed, spinning-disk reads, endpoint writes, USB adapters, updates and concurrent encryption are unknown. For illustration only, 14 separate transfers of 150 GB total 2.1 TB: theoretical wire time is about 280 minutes over a shared 1 Gb/s link or 28 minutes over 10 Gb/s, before overhead or setup. Actual compression and multicast alter this workload; six images may require separate groups. Switch port count does not establish unlimited capacity.

Pilot results determine the committed threshold. Failure to meet the target requires an explicit scope, time or equipment decision, not a claim of success.

### Acceptance evidence

Test all six image variants, with at least one physical example of each model. Include available DDR4/DDR5 variants and adapter types; document untested variants. Expand from single-device runs to small groups, then representative up-to-14-device batches when equipment is available. A smaller test does not certify capacity for 14. Retain individual timings and failures; no fabricated percentile claims from small samples.

## 5. Project Solution Overview

| Module | Priority | Outcome |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Network boot and imaging | P0 | Correct approved image restored with Secure Boot enabled |
| Six-image catalog | P0 | Model and Office choice visible to technician |
| Post-deployment setup | P0 | Named, configured, updated, encrypted endpoint |
| Automated Entra join investigation | P0 | Supported, licensed method demonstrated before architecture is finalized |
| Unique identities / recovery verification | P0 | No copied enrollment, agent or recovery identities |
| Name validation | P0 | 15-character limit and technician approval of adjustments |
| Cross-system duplicate-name lookup | P1 | FOG plus authorized Entra lookup; capability subject to permissions |
| Backups, logs, runbook and training | P0 | Maintainable and recoverable operation |
| SharePoint / CSV integration | Future | Not required in initial release |

FOG supplies image management and deployment; Windows automation supplies the validated post-deployment sequence. Use existing management interfaces and scripts where practical; this PRD does not require a new web application.

The initial release must cover the complete deployment and validation process. Automated Entra joining is a required investigation and desired release capability. A manual fallback has not been accepted by the stakeholder and cannot silently replace it. Release disposition is an explicit decision if a supported method is unavailable with current entitlements.

## 6. Project Scope

Included: the R720 service; approved PXE/DHCP configuration; three model families; six Windows image variants; required applications; naming; Entra-only joining; full encryption completion; updates and cleanup; used-device preflight; deployment evidence; monthly image lifecycle; external backup; runbook and training.

| System | Interaction | Owner |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ubuntu / FOG / RAID | Hosting, images and deployment | Maintenance owner |
| Office network | Boot services, addressing and traffic | Network administrator / Director; assignment TBD |
| Entra | Device join, identity and recovery-key association | Tenant administrator; assignment TBD |
| Webroot / Resolve | Per-device registration and checks | IT Support / console administrators |
| Microsoft / vendor services | Activation, updates and downloads | IT Support; entitlement owner TBD |
| External drives | Former-employee preservation and separate FOG backup | Maintenance owner |

Out of scope: Active Directory domain joining, SharePoint/CSV integration, new purchases without a separate decision, universal cross-model image, remote-office/WAN deployment, unlimited concurrency, and automated installation of one-off paid Acrobat.

Former-employee data remains on the existing separate backup workflow. Do not place personal data in standard deployment images.

## 7. Project Risks

Likelihood is unmeasured; these are investigation priorities.

| Risk | Impact | Response / owner |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Enrollment method requires unavailable entitlements or incompatible tenant policies | Automation blocked | Tenant admin proves supported method before release; escalate alternative to stakeholder |
| FOG release / Ubuntu / Secure Boot / USB adapter mismatch | Cannot image while preserving Secure Boot | Maintenance owner validates exact versions and hardware; do not disable Secure Boot as a workaround |
| Disk/network/encryption bottleneck | 45–60-minute target missed | Instrument pilot, compare same/mixed-image groups, set evidence-based commitment |
| Copied identity, activation or BitLocker state | Records overwritten or unusable recovery keys | Generalized, unjoined reference; vendor-supported agent staging; fresh device provisioning and checks |
| Incorrect image, name or erasure target | Data loss / deployment error | Service Tag confirmation, image compatibility checks, verified backup and explicit task authorization |
| Six monthly images or backup rotation incomplete | Stale or unrecoverable deployment | Version manifest, tests, checksums and restore exercise before retirement |
| Office 2016 past support | Unsupported software exposure | Retain per stakeholder requirement; Director documents disposition; no migration assumed |
| Scope growth / single maintainer | Missed readiness target or operational gap | Defer integrations, train another technician where possible, retain runbook |

Constraints: existing equipment/licenses, Secure Boot stays on, standard employees, completed encryption, Entra-only identity. Assumptions requiring proof: adequate network access, compatible boot chain, suitable activation rights/methods, available pilot devices, and sufficient usable backup capacity. RAID 1 does not replace a backup.

## 8. Terms and Abbreviations

| Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| FOG | Network imaging and deployment platform |
| PXE | Network boot mechanism |
| Multicast | Shared image stream for a compatible group receiving the same image |
| Sysprep/generalize | Windows preparation to remove machine-specific setup state for image deployment |
| Entra join | Device identity in Microsoft Entra ID; distinct from local AD join and Intune management |
| Provisioning package | Windows configuration package; candidate enrollment vehicle after validation |
| LPT | Company acronym in prefix; also laptop type suffix |
| PC | Desktop suffix |
| Ready for validation | Deployment configuration finished, including encryption; technician tests still pending |
| Approved for handoff | Separate technician tests passed |
| Service Tag | Dell hardware identifier, used to distinguish devices independently of shared adapters |

